  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with a custom-shader water scene. It passes the typecheck, but I couldn't run it in a browser, so none of the visuals or the GLSL compile have been checked. The water is a 360x360 grid (240 units wide) displaced in the vertex shader by four summed sine waves, with the normal computed analytically. The fragment shader adds noise-based ripples that perturb the normal and fade with distance, a Schlick fresnel mix between water body colour and reflected sky, and a sun glint. A sky dome (a large back-facing sphere with a gradient and sun) uses the same sky function as the water reflection. The water fades to the horizon colour at distance so its edge meets the dome. Time comes from a `waveClock` uniform updated every frame. The camera sits about 3 units above the water looking toward the horizon. The shaders output linear colour, so the engine's default ACES filmic tone mapping applies to the sky and water alike. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frame callback. I avoided top-level `await` because the default Vite build target would reject it.",
